5
00:00:19,119 --> 00:00:21,109
Welcome to Software Engineering Radio.

6
00:00:21,119 --> 00:00:23,590
I'm your host Jeff Doolittle.

7
00:00:23,590 --> 00:00:29,029
This episode will explore the role of human programmers in the AI era.

8
00:00:29,029 --> 00:00:33,670
Topics around machine learning and AI have come up frequently in previous episodes.

9
00:00:33,670 --> 00:00:43,030
Some examples are episode 633 with Itamar Freriedman on automated testing with generative AI.

10
00:00:43,030 --> 00:00:49,110
Episode 63 hosted Rish Singh on using Genai for test code generation.

11
00:00:49,110 --> 00:00:57,270
And in episode 626, EPZ Oskaya joined software engineering radio again to discuss Gen AI for software architecture.

12
00:00:57,270 --> 00:01:04,149
Today we're hosting Arty Chevchenko who is a former Google software engineer, exceed and founder of a startup.

13
00:01:04,149 --> 00:01:13,270
Currently he's a software engineer at Canva and a lecturer at ITMO University which is famous for winning more programming world cups than any other university in the world.

14
00:01:13,270 --> 00:01:23,031
He is also the author of the recently published book Code Health Guardian, the old new role of human programmers in the AI era. Arty, welcome to the show.

15
00:01:23,040 --> 00:01:25,511
Thanks for having me. Glad you're here.

16
00:01:25,520 --> 00:01:33,109
The subtitle of your book, as I said, is the old new role of human programmers in the AI era.

17
00:01:33,109 --> 00:01:38,230
In your view, what does it take for programmers to be successful in the era of AI?

18
00:01:38,240 --> 00:01:43,670
I think there are three things you need to be great at.

19
00:01:43,670 --> 00:01:46,951
One is collaborating with AI. Another is delegating to AI.

20
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:51,590
These two are very much on the hype right now.

21
00:01:51,590 --> 00:01:54,310
Everybody talks about Copilot and Cursor, I guess.

22
00:01:54,310 --> 00:01:57,109
But there is also one more thing there.

23
00:01:57,109 --> 00:02:00,550
You really want to be better than AI at something.

24
00:02:00,550 --> 00:02:09,749
And if we were talking about software engineers, I would say your best bet is to become an expert in managing code complexity.

25
00:02:09,749 --> 00:02:18,150
Just because complex reasoning is hard for AI and in software engineering, that's probably the hardest problem we have.

26
00:02:18,150 --> 00:02:20,949
How do we keep our code bases reasonably simple?

27
00:02:20,949 --> 00:02:29,030
So yeah, honestly I wouldn't over focus on learning these AI tools like cursor and copilot today

28
00:02:29,030 --> 00:02:40,150
and really spend some time to make sure that you're ready for this new role of human software engineers which is code health guardian that's how I call it and that's the title of the book.

29
00:02:40,150 --> 00:02:42,710
So you mentioned the idea of complexity.

30
00:02:42,710 --> 00:02:44,790
In case it's unclear to our listeners, help us connect.

31
00:02:44,790 --> 00:02:50,949
How does complexity relate to this idea that you describe about programmers as code health guardians?

32
00:02:50,959 --> 00:02:52,949
Yeah, definitely.

33
00:02:52,949 --> 00:03:01,110
When I say code health, I basically mean code that is simple enough, code that is reasonably simple or maintainable.

34
00:03:01,120 --> 00:03:08,869
So if that's the hardest problem in software engineering to keep our code bases simple enough then it's likely

35
00:03:08,879 --> 00:03:19,110
that we will continue to be better than AI in this for at least some time maybe years maybe even decades so yeah that's

36
00:03:19,120 --> 00:03:33,430
basically the link there why does AI struggle to keep things simple in your experience complex reasoning is hard for AI so it looks simple on the surface often I I think that's what AI is very good at.

37
00:03:33,430 --> 00:03:38,550
It gives you an impression that it understands things, right?

38
00:03:38,550 --> 00:03:42,789
And it gives you an impression that okay, that's super simple.

39
00:03:42,789 --> 00:03:46,470
It's like 1 2 3 here you go.

40
00:03:46,470 --> 00:03:58,470
But sometimes the real world has very significant challenges that need to be deeply understood and that's what humans still do better than AI at this point.

41
00:03:58,470 --> 00:04:04,231
So would you say sometimes the AI comes up with simplistic solutions rather than simple ones? Okay. Okay.

42
00:04:04,231 --> 00:04:15,429
So your contention is that humans still need to maintain the sense of complexity management and the way we do it is by guarding the health of our code.

43
00:04:15,439 --> 00:04:18,150
Yeah. Exactly. And you mentioned maintainability.

44
00:04:18,150 --> 00:04:20,469
Are there other aspects to code health?

45
00:04:20,469 --> 00:04:23,670
You mentioned simplicity and maintainability.

46
00:04:23,670 --> 00:04:26,710
How else would you characterize code that is healthy?

47
00:04:26,720 --> 00:04:40,230
That's basically the definition. If we're talking about definitions, I just use the term health as an alternative to saying that is reasonably simple or pragmatically simple.

48
00:04:40,230 --> 00:04:44,790
But I like to think about code health as a productivity question.

49
00:04:44,790 --> 00:04:47,749
I think it is ultimately a productivity question.

50
00:04:47,749 --> 00:04:56,950
So if you think about human productivity or programmers productivity, the first thing that comes to mind is team productivity, right?

51
00:04:56,950 --> 00:05:04,950
There are a bunch of factors that make team either productive or not productive like psychological safety,

52
00:05:04,950 --> 00:05:10,550
reliability, clear goals, clear responsibilities, impact, all these kind of things.

53
00:05:10,550 --> 00:05:19,749
But if we zoom in a little bit into an individual in a team, still in a reasonably productive team,

54
00:05:19,749 --> 00:05:24,150
you will have individuals which perform really great and individuals that perform not that great.

55
00:05:24,150 --> 00:05:34,629
And historically, if you're an engineering manager dealing with this issue, the question you're asking yourself is, well,

56
00:05:34,629 --> 00:05:38,550
is it a skill problem or is it a will problem, right?

57
00:05:38,550 --> 00:05:46,710
But I find that often the answer to this question is no. It may be all good with skills and it may be all good with the will.

58
00:05:46,710 --> 00:05:55,270
So for me, I don't think I ever had any major issues with the will problems or skill problems.

59
00:05:55,270 --> 00:06:02,310
For me, I definitely had some issues with finding the right balance between long-term and short-term productivity.

60
00:06:02,320 --> 00:06:07,350
And that's what I call term productivity.

61
00:06:07,350 --> 00:06:09,749
It's pretty hard to talk without any visuals.

62
00:06:09,749 --> 00:06:20,150
But if you can imagine having a spectrum where on the left side it is short-term productivity and on the right side it is long-term productivity.

63
00:06:20,150 --> 00:06:26,629
If you're too focused on the long-term productivity, it's kind of obvious why you're not productive because you're often planning to be productive in the future, right?

64
00:06:26,639 --> 00:06:28,230
Someday I'll be productive, right?

65
00:06:28,240 --> 00:06:31,110
Yeah.

66
00:06:31,110 --> 00:06:36,150
And uh if you're focused too much on the short term, you become too tactical.

67
00:06:36,150 --> 00:06:41,590
you create too much complexity along the way and that's what AI agents could do for us too.

68
00:06:41,590 --> 00:06:43,270
And you really want to have the balance there.

69
00:06:43,270 --> 00:06:56,790
You want to care about users needs right now to deliver now but also care about user needs like a year from now so that you don't create too much complexity so that you cannot execute in a year from now.

70
00:06:56,800 --> 00:06:58,550
Right.

71
00:06:58,550 --> 00:07:00,710
So a few interesting insights from that one.

72
00:07:00,710 --> 00:07:02,870
You didn't seem to say that long-term or short-term is better.

73
00:07:02,880 --> 00:07:07,350
There's a balance between long and short-term productivity. Did I understand that correctly?

74
00:07:07,360 --> 00:07:09,189
Yeah, that's correct.

75
00:07:09,189 --> 00:07:16,550
And definitely there are times when you need to focus on the short term for several weeks or maybe even months and just deliver.

76
00:07:16,560 --> 00:07:26,550
There are times when you need to focus more on long-term, but in general, you need to find the healthy balance between these two extremes and be somewhere in the middle.

77
00:07:26,560 --> 00:07:28,790
Okay.

78
00:07:28,790 --> 00:07:36,870
And then the other aspect I think you're implying as well is that it's humans job to ensure long-term productivity.

79
00:07:36,870 --> 00:07:45,511
That AI might make us short-term productive, but that at least as it currently stands, it's not going to solve the problem for us of long-term productivity. Is that correct?

80
00:07:45,520 --> 00:07:48,309
Yeah, exactly. Okay, great.

81
00:07:48,309 --> 00:07:55,029
Well, that might lead really well into my next question, which is what motivated you to write Code Health Guardian?

82
00:07:55,029 --> 00:08:00,869
Well, I guess I was always passionate about knowledge sharing.

83
00:08:00,869 --> 00:08:04,309
So, even starting with my uni years,

84
00:08:04,309 --> 00:08:16,550
I remember we were doing classes for high school students and even primary school students a little bit and right now I'm teaching in university doing university classes.

85
00:08:16,550 --> 00:08:21,909
So, yeah, this passion for knowledge sharing was pretty much always there.

86
00:08:21,909 --> 00:08:24,629
But in terms of this particular book,

87
00:08:24,629 --> 00:08:37,269
I guess there are three key events there apart from doing university classes which definitely prepared me for writing this book because they inspired the content of those classes inspired the content of the book very very much.

88
00:08:37,269 --> 00:08:41,029
But there are two other key events I would mention.

89
00:08:41,039 --> 00:08:48,150
The first one is doing good code reviewer internal classes at Google.

90
00:08:48,150 --> 00:08:53,670
So the story there is that when I was at Google,

91
00:08:53,670 --> 00:09:08,550
I was pretty much every day I was just spending a little bit of time reading random documentation and at some point I started realizing that there is so much information about how to do code reviews on our internet and I just wanted to consolidate all of that.

92
00:09:08,550 --> 00:09:10,310
So that's what I did.

93
00:09:10,310 --> 00:09:15,430
I just consolidated all of that, created a single slide deck for that.

94
00:09:15,430 --> 00:09:28,310
And at some point that slide deck became pretty popular and I even started getting requests from other Googlers asking whether there are any tech talks about this, if there are any classes about this.

95
00:09:28,310 --> 00:09:35,990
So I started doing the good code reviewer classes internally at Google and that was pretty successful.

96
00:09:35,990 --> 00:09:43,190
I think in just one year we had over a thousand Googlers, software engineers who went through those classes.

97
00:09:43,190 --> 00:09:50,870
We got like astonishing 4.5 rating based on over 100 reviews and that's really golden standard at Google for tech talks.

98
00:09:50,870 --> 00:10:01,350
Like you know when we're doing on boarding that's roughly the rating you get for those on boarding classes and like if you can if you can reach 4.5 that's kind of that means you've done a good job.

99
00:10:01,360 --> 00:10:04,070
Yeah.

100
00:10:04,070 --> 00:10:07,110
So yeah, that was the first key event doing those classes.

101
00:10:07,110 --> 00:10:12,470
It gave me quite a lot of confidence I guess to start university courses later.

102
00:10:12,470 --> 00:10:21,190
The second key event was John Asterhood book presentation in Google's Sunnyville office.

103
00:10:21,190 --> 00:10:24,310
So I just randomly ended up being there.

104
00:10:24,310 --> 00:10:26,949
I had no idea what to expect.

105
00:10:26,949 --> 00:10:36,949
I guess it was just advertised by some internal channels and I ended up being in that room just knowing that some guy is going to present on his software engineering book.

106
00:10:36,949 --> 00:10:46,230
And when he was talking about software engineering and complexity and complexity causes and complexity symptoms as he called them,

107
00:10:46,240 --> 00:10:56,630
that really resonated with me and was like, "Oh yeah, that really makes sense." And yeah then when I was starting the university classes I think

108
00:10:56,640 --> 00:11:07,190
to a large extent maybe up to 50% the initial content of those university courses was actually based on John's book philosophy of software design.

109
00:11:07,190 --> 00:11:14,550
Also of course my own experience at Google and a bunch of other books like pragmatic programmer but John's book was very very key.

110
00:11:14,560 --> 00:11:16,310
That's great.

111
00:11:16,310 --> 00:11:25,590
And I'll put a link in the show notes to software engineering radio episode 520 where I interviewed John Osterhout about his book a philosophy of software design.

112
00:11:25,590 --> 00:11:28,230
So that'll be good for listeners to be able to dig into.

113
00:11:28,230 --> 00:11:35,590
So a lot of our guests on the show have written books and my understanding having not published a book myself is that it can be challenging.

114
00:11:35,590 --> 00:11:37,990
So what helped you to stick with it along the way?

115
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:41,430
Yeah, it was a long long process.

116
00:11:41,430 --> 00:11:45,350
I started it in 2022.

117
00:11:45,350 --> 00:11:53,030
At that point, software engineering at Google book was already published and a bunch of things that I've learned internally at Google.

118
00:11:53,040 --> 00:12:05,430
I was surprised that they were not covered because I was expecting it to be a lot about actually coding and software design and all this stuff how we do this internally at Google.

119
00:12:05,430 --> 00:12:09,350
But if you take a look at the Flamingo book, there is not much about it there.

120
00:12:09,350 --> 00:12:11,829
It's mostly about building engineering organization.

121
00:12:11,839 --> 00:12:17,829
Okay. So that gave me a feeling that there is a an opportunity there.

122
00:12:17,829 --> 00:12:22,069
Already at that point I been iterating on the university classes for quite a long time.

123
00:12:22,069 --> 00:12:35,110
So I've had quite a lot of insights into how to communicate complex topics in a simple way but also some unique insights about software design as well.

124
00:12:35,110 --> 00:12:40,389
And in 2022 when I suddenly got quite a lot of time I decided to start writing the book.

125
00:12:40,389 --> 00:12:47,910
So I did a couple of chapters and one of the chapters was on design docs.

126
00:12:47,910 --> 00:12:56,150
So I reached out to Max Canada Alexander the author of code simplicity with that draft asking for his feedback.

127
00:12:56,150 --> 00:13:05,430
And I guess that's one of the turning points when you want to get support from somebody because you're kind of like you're just starting and you're not completely sure how will it go.

128
00:13:05,430 --> 00:13:14,230
And what Max did he actually referred me to Titus Winters who was saying look for this you probably need to talk to Titus.

129
00:13:14,230 --> 00:13:18,470
He was curating software engineering at Google and he can give you a feedback for this.

130
00:13:18,480 --> 00:13:19,990
So that's what I did.

131
00:13:19,990 --> 00:13:22,389
I asked him for a warm intro.

132
00:13:22,389 --> 00:13:28,230
He did me a warm winter and I sent it to Titus and Titus was so supportive.

133
00:13:28,230 --> 00:13:33,829
He looked through the chapter draft and said that well look it looks great.

134
00:13:33,829 --> 00:13:40,389
For different reasons, we decided to keep the design docs section in software engineer and Google pretty short.

135
00:13:40,389 --> 00:13:49,269
But yeah, it's definitely worth making it public and talking about this to which I responded to him saying like, "Hey, do you want to write this together,

136
00:13:49,269 --> 00:13:56,949
write this book together, maybe overly ambitious, but at that point I already went through the YC combinator startup school and was running a startup.

137
00:13:56,949 --> 00:14:00,310
So like I was a little bit pushy, but Titus said like that would be great.

138
00:14:00,310 --> 00:14:02,790
That's a great project, but I don't really have time for this.

139
00:14:02,800 --> 00:14:08,550
Anyway, Titus support was very important in the beginning.

140
00:14:08,550 --> 00:14:14,150
I guess that what made me brave enough to continue working on it.

141
00:14:14,150 --> 00:14:25,750
I guess the second turning point was when after a year of writing, I realized that the scope is a little bit too big, you know, because originally the name of the book was productive software engineering.

142
00:14:25,750 --> 00:14:28,629
That's what I wanted to That's the name of the university.

143
00:14:28,629 --> 00:14:31,189
Broad it is.

144
00:14:31,189 --> 00:14:36,629
Yeah, but that's the university course and I was thinking maybe I I would just publish my university course and it has two parts.

145
00:14:36,629 --> 00:14:46,230
It has this technical part which is about code complexity and there is like nontechnical part but after a year I realized yeah that's too much.

146
00:14:46,230 --> 00:14:53,750
So it would be talmud if if you can say it in English right that's the word yeah a very very long book so I've reduced the scope

147
00:14:53,750 --> 00:15:03,189
and decided to focus solely on code health and decided let it be a story of a healthy codebase so that was the second part

148
00:15:03,189 --> 00:15:15,990
that really helped actually complete this work and the third one the third turning point not a turning point but I received a lot of support from tech reviewers So that's something I didn't expect,

149
00:15:15,990 --> 00:15:24,870
but there were so many like dozens of people who left a bunch of comments on the manuscript and helped to make this book much better.

150
00:15:24,870 --> 00:15:27,269
So yeah, I guess these three events or turning points.

151
00:15:27,279 --> 00:15:29,430
Great.

152
00:15:29,430 --> 00:15:36,069
Can you share a real world example of a project or a team that neglected code health and it led to significant productivity issues?

153
00:15:36,079 --> 00:15:38,550
Yeah, sure.

154
00:15:38,550 --> 00:15:51,189
I think actually these stories is what made me truly pragmatic and truly care about code health to be like strategic about code complexity not on one of the extremes on the term productivity spectrum

155
00:15:51,189 --> 00:15:59,670
and that's story is from my startup from the company I was running I think that was really really motivating not only

156
00:15:59,670 --> 00:16:08,710
because I as a CEO cared about product so much and productivity but also that because it was a self-funded startup.

157
00:16:08,710 --> 00:16:13,910
So, I was actually paying engineers to do the work.

158
00:16:13,910 --> 00:16:19,670
So, if you see having productivity issues, you have really good incentives to fix it.

159
00:16:19,670 --> 00:16:21,990
And we had problems on both sides of the spectrum.

160
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:33,189
We definitely had problems on the to clean code approach like more kind of a long-term investing in the long-term productivity

161
00:16:33,189 --> 00:16:40,470
when both on the back end I was spending too much time on stuff that didn't matter that much especially in the startup world where you need to iterate

162
00:16:40,470 --> 00:16:54,310
so fast but on the front end side I also saw front end engineers building some sort of frameworks and reinventing wheels and being kind of strategic about what they're doing but That's not what we wanted and we needed to iterate.

163
00:16:54,310 --> 00:17:00,230
We needed to get feedback from customers very quickly.

164
00:17:00,230 --> 00:17:01,990
So yeah, that's on that side of the spectrum.

165
00:17:01,990 --> 00:17:05,750
But interestingly, we had problems on the other side of the spectrum as well.

166
00:17:05,750 --> 00:17:09,029
So sometimes they would just iterate and deliver stuff.

167
00:17:09,039 --> 00:17:19,829
But after a year I think or so front end engineers came to me and said like one of the key pages I wanted changes on, they just couldn't do that. They just couldn't deliver that.

168
00:17:19,829 --> 00:17:23,351
They were just telling me, you know, like it's too complicated. I'm doing something.

169
00:17:23,351 --> 00:17:26,630
I'm breaking something and I don't really understand what I'm doing anymore.

170
00:17:26,630 --> 00:17:28,230
And we had to start from the beginning.

171
00:17:28,230 --> 00:17:32,230
So, we had problems on each side of the spectrum, which was pretty interesting.

172
00:17:32,240 --> 00:17:34,070
Yeah, that's fascinating.

173
00:17:34,070 --> 00:17:40,710
So, you're basically saying there was sometimes too much long-term thinking and too much short-term thinking at the same time.

174
00:17:40,720 --> 00:17:43,750
Yeah, I guess in in different parts, in different projects.

175
00:17:43,760 --> 00:17:45,431
In different parts. Yeah.

176
00:17:45,440 --> 00:17:49,350
And so there's a balance you have to strike.

177
00:17:49,350 --> 00:17:51,110
You need some long-term strategic thinking.

178
00:17:51,110 --> 00:17:56,070
You need some short-term iterating, but you have to apply it appropriately in the appropriate contexts.

179
00:17:56,080 --> 00:17:58,630
Yeah, you that's a tricky balance.

180
00:17:58,640 --> 00:18:01,990
It is a tricky balance. How do you strike that balance?

181
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:09,029
I believe the easiest way and how it worked for me is the ownership mentality.

182
00:18:09,029 --> 00:18:12,710
Honestly, ah well that's the other thing I was going to highlight that you said it was your money.

183
00:18:12,720 --> 00:18:15,270
Yeah, exactly. So tell Yeah.

184
00:18:15,270 --> 00:18:18,470
So tell us more about that. That's interesting. What exactly do you want to hear?

185
00:18:18,480 --> 00:18:26,950
Well, you know, I had a mentor of mine tell me that if you want to increase your engineering outcomes, you should treat the company's money like it's your own money.

186
00:18:26,950 --> 00:18:29,190
And it sounds like you're basically saying the same thing.

187
00:18:29,200 --> 00:18:33,830
So, how did it change things for you when it was your money?

188
00:18:33,840 --> 00:18:39,830
Yeah, as I mentioned, you care about productivity not only because of the product.

189
00:18:39,830 --> 00:18:43,350
You start caring about productivity also because you're actually spending money on this.

190
00:18:43,350 --> 00:18:51,510
I don't think you can apply this to many software engineers because they're not paying their money for what they're doing.

191
00:18:51,510 --> 00:19:00,870
But I think what is realistic to expect is still to have this ownership mentality like be kind of a product owners.

192
00:19:00,870 --> 00:19:08,789
I think that's the only way to truly strike the balance there because if we're saying okay and I've read it somewhere too like it's

193
00:19:08,789 --> 00:19:16,710
uh product manager's job to care about the product and it's software engineer's job to care about code quality so that you can continue iterating in the future

194
00:19:16,710 --> 00:19:25,590
right but I don't think it's a perfectly valid statement because once you do that it's kind of a distributed responsibility

195
00:19:25,590 --> 00:19:33,990
and you have this push and pull and depends on who pushes harder and it's like it's pretty tricky because product managers wouldn't have half of the story

196
00:19:33,990 --> 00:19:43,190
right they're not exposed to the code base they cannot reason about the tech depth and all that stuff so I think the only good solution here is

197
00:19:43,190 --> 00:19:53,750
for engineers to be more product focused to be more like a product owners and engineers have much higher chances of having a full picture do you have an example of an engineer

