00:43:01,910 --> 00:43:04,870
I think it goes all the way back to Paras's definition.

439
00:43:04,880 --> 00:43:07,030
It does.

440
00:43:07,030 --> 00:43:11,750
A module is something with an interface inputs outputs and inside it should be a black box.

441
00:43:11,750 --> 00:43:14,870
So it could be a function and it could be a system and anything in between.

442
00:43:14,880 --> 00:43:18,470
Yeah.

443
00:43:18,470 --> 00:43:26,390
But then if you want to models to be useful, if you want your decomposition to not look like fu which calls fu 1,

444
00:43:26,390 --> 00:43:36,470
fu 2, fu 3, fu 4, like you really need to decompose it meaningfully and then have clear contracts for the other modules that you're calling from the implementation.

445
00:43:36,470 --> 00:43:41,589
So that's where design by contract really comes into play.

446
00:43:41,589 --> 00:43:49,270
It's kind of obvious things, but it's about being explicit about what the module does,

447
00:43:49,270 --> 00:43:59,910
documenting whatever is missing from the code, just writing down as documentation and then internally enforcing those contracts internally in the module.

448
00:43:59,910 --> 00:44:04,630
So if you provide a class that has an interface,

449
00:44:04,630 --> 00:44:13,030
it is your responsibility to actually test that implementation that it actually does what the interface promises to do.

450
00:44:13,030 --> 00:44:15,589
So that's basically the crux of the design contract.

451
00:44:15,589 --> 00:44:21,750
You document what this model is doing and you internally enforce it.

452
00:44:21,750 --> 00:44:25,190
It's only not only about tests.

453
00:44:25,190 --> 00:44:27,109
Assertions also is a huge part of it.

454
00:44:27,109 --> 00:44:30,550
They're especially useful for enforcing preconditions.

455
00:44:30,560 --> 00:44:43,750
Design a contract as a term was coined by Beton Mayor when he was working on his AEL language and he used the terms precondition, postcond condition and cluster I believe.

456
00:44:43,750 --> 00:44:58,230
So basically the way he was putting it is that if all the preconditions are met then the module guarantees that all the post conditions will be met after the call and that all the invariance would still hold.

457
00:44:58,230 --> 00:45:10,150
So basically that's I think roughly the definition he was given and yeah when we're talking about assertions they in particular help with validating the preconditions in runtime.

458
00:45:10,150 --> 00:45:21,750
I think uh pragmatic programmer makes a very good point about assertions saying that it is naive to rely on tests only because yes

459
00:45:21,760 --> 00:45:29,829
you need good test coverage and that's definitely the main contract enforcement mechanism

460
00:45:29,829 --> 00:45:37,910
but apart from tests there are billions of different states your program will go through in production in runtime.

461
00:45:37,910 --> 00:45:41,829
So having assertions in runtime actually is complimentary to having good test coverage.

462
00:45:41,839 --> 00:45:46,790
That's interesting. You said there could be billions of states your system could be in.

463
00:45:46,790 --> 00:45:51,270
So effectively it sounds like you're making the case that assertions are another strategy for handling complexity.

464
00:45:51,280 --> 00:45:53,750
Yeah, exactly.

465
00:45:53,750 --> 00:46:02,710
It's it's a strategy for enforcing contracts that at least all the preconditions are really met in your production system, right?

466
00:46:02,710 --> 00:46:07,590
and reducing the number of possible states that your system can be in to ones that you are aware of. Yeah.

467
00:46:07,599 --> 00:46:10,150
Yeah.

468
00:46:10,150 --> 00:46:16,230
It's if you do not do that, you will end up with having extra logic handling those edge cases.

